# DEFECT 2558: the runtime's null arm killed a fault a library's own handler
# would recover (panel 249's ffi-pragmatist and compiler-engineer, each apart,
# 2026-11-15).
#
# A Go c-archive installs its fault handler from its constructor, before
# `main`, and turns a nil dereference into a panic its `recover` catches by
# editing the faulting context into a call of `runtime.sigpanic`. The guard
# the runtime installs in `main` found that handler and saved it, then claimed
# every fault under its null window as the program's own before handing it
# on: a Heroes program binding such an archive died at its first recovered
# nil dereference, exit 134, `a null pointer was read through, at offset 0x0,
# called from main.GoNilPanic`, three runs of three on this Mac and in Linux
# arm64. Now a fault at a low address that ran off the stack the runtime
# measured, on a thread made ready for signals of its own, goes to the handler
# before the runtime's first, and the program goes on where it sent it.
#
# The library here does what Go does, on a thread of its own holding an
# alternate stack as every thread that runs Go code does: it steps over a null
# read, returns 9 from a call to address zero, and jumps out of a second null
# read, each by its own handler; then it steps over the first again, the guard
# still in place (`fixedbugs-2558-library.h`).

extern "fixedbugs-2558-library.h"
    function lib_recovered(which: i64) -> i64

function main()
    print("a read stepped over: ", lib_recovered(which: 0))
    print("a call returned from: ", lib_recovered(which: 1))
    print("a read jumped out of: ", lib_recovered(which: 2))
    print("the first again: ", lib_recovered(which: 0))
